motion-lab-works-build-question-2 = Holding the thread by its loop over the centre of a 15 cm circle, we swing the body round it and time { $turns } turns. From that we find the period, the frequency and the body’s speed. Then we shorten the thread and keep the circle. How does the period change?
motion-lab-works-answer-rotation-period-why = The body goes round because the slanting thread pulls it not only up but also towards the centre. The circle is the same, but a short thread slants more, so a bigger share of the pull points to the centre — and the body has to go round faster for that pull to keep it just on the circle. It comes out T = 2π·√(h/g), h being the hand’s height above the circle: a shorter thread — the hand lower over the circle — a shorter period. It is the same law as for a thread pendulum — because it is a pendulum too, only it turns instead of swinging.
motion-lab-works-answer-rotation-period-say = A shorter thread — quicker turns!
motion-lab-works-answer-rotation-period-text = With a { $L } thread { $N } turns took { $t }: T = t/N ≈ { $value }, n = N/t ≈ { $value2 } rev/s, the body’s speed v = 2πR/T ≈ { $value3 } m/s.{" "}
motion-lab-works-answer-rotation-period-text-2 = With a { $L } thread — { $t }: T ≈ { $value }.{" "}
motion-lab-works-answer-rotation-period-text-3 = { $L }{ $L2 }The circle is the same, but a shorter thread slants more, and to go round the circle the body has to turn more often: the period depends on the thread (for a body on a thread T = 2π·√(h/g), h being the hand’s height above the circle). That is why the period is timed over many turns: one turn takes about a second, and a hand starts the stopwatch ≈ 0.1–0.2 s late.
motion-lab-works-pendulum-ask = What does a pendulum’s period depend on: the amplitude, the ball’s mass or the length?
motion-lab-works-pendulum-title = The swings of a thread pendulum
