motion-lab-works-answer-average-speed-text = The ball covered the whole path l = { $value } in { $whole }: v = l/t ≈ { $v1 }. The first half — in { $half }: v ≈ { $v2 }. The ball keeps speeding up, so it covers the second half faster than the first, and the average speed on the whole path is greater — here { $v12 }. So an average speed always belongs to a stretch of the path. The stopwatch here reads exactly; by hand one starts and stops it ≈ 0.1–0.2 s late — that is why the textbook repeats the run three times and takes the mean time.
